ressions are not  to be measured by their results by the  the event of what happens right but  rather by the vices of the person who is  engaging in them and likewise with with  virtues so this is assuming that there  is you know vices in the person who is  doing wrong or a vice some sort of  defect right and that there is a virtue  you in somebody who is good and he says  that it does not make a difference a  relevant difference it doesn't mean that  we can't make distinctions but there is  no relevant difference based on the  importance of the situation you know he  says there's no mayest and meanness  greater and lesser that that matters  here there is a greater and lesser but  it just doesn't matter in the fact that  we are doing the wrong thing and the  effects are the consequences themselves  well they do exist are not relevant to  determining whether this is  fundamentally good or bad he provides  some very interesting examples here he  says if you capsize the ship that you  are steering with a cargo of gold  bullion or a cargo of chaff it doesn't  really make any difference it makes it  make difference in the result right  somebody is out a lot of money whereas  somebody just lost some some chaff but  it's an equal mistake so as far as the  Stoics are concerned the one person is  just as culpable as is the other here's  another example passion has made a slip  in the case of a woman of no position in  this case resentment extends to fewer  persons that if it had played the wanton  with some maiden who is a lady of high  birth right so we could transpose this  to the present right somebody is in a  relationship with with somebody else and  there's not a lot of you know stakes in  it but they do cheat on them right  because they're motivated by desire  now imagine somebody who is in a  marriage with children and there's all  sorts of you know family connections  going on and there are political  implications to this and they decide to  screw around right it's 